import mundane theorem    stdlib/reasoning/modus-ponens

theorem peirce
  metamath    peirce
  note        Suppose a = 0 fails. Then "if a = 0 then b = 0" holds because
              its condition cannot, and the hypothesis turns that into a = 0,
              which contradicts the supposition.
  let a ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  let b ∈ ℝ                                                           (H2)
  then if (if (if a = 0 then b = 0) then a = 0) then a = 0

1.  If (if (if a = 0 then b = 0) then a = 0) then a = 0.
    proof
    assume if (if a = 0 then b = 0) then a = 0                        (K1)

    1.1.  a = 0
          contradiction
          suppose a ≠ 0                                               (S)

          1.1.1.  If a = 0 then b = 0.
                  proof
                  assume a = 0                                        (K2)

                  1.1.1.1.  a ≠ 0
                            restate S, contradicting K2

          1.1.2.  a = 0
                  mun:modus-ponens, from K1, 1.1.1, contradicting S
